A property is useful when direct assignment also needs one controlled validation path.

```python
class Thermostat:
    @property
    def temperature(self):
        return self._temperature

    @temperature.setter
    def temperature(self, value):
        if value < 5:
            value = 5
        self._temperature = value
```

Callers still write `thermostat.temperature = value`, but the setter controls what is actually stored.

The backing attribute keeps the validation logic from recursively assigning the property to itself.
